    "body": "Makes `btck_LogEntry` an opaque type with `btck_log_entry_get_*` accessors, instead of a public struct. This allows adding or changing fields without breaking the ABI, and is consistent with ~all other kernel objects. Follow-up to https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/34374#discussion_r4207440088. \n\nI added a benchmark in https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/compare/master...stickies-v:bitcoin:2026-10/kernel-logentry-bench, and found that performance is not meaningfully impacted either way. Some results below.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Performance stats</summary>\n\n### Performance\n\nFields are now converted only when read, at the cost of a library call per field. The [benchmark](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/compare/master...stickies-v:bitcoin:2026-10/kernel-logentry-bench) measures a kernel log statement up to and including the callback (Apple M4 Pro, static library, `-O2`, median of 2×25 interleaved runs):\n\n| Callback reads | master | PR | Delta |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| no fields | 57.4 ns | 55.2 ns | −1.6 to −2.5 ns |\n| message only | 57.8 ns | 56.0 ns | −1.4 to −2.0 ns |\n| all 9 fields | 57.9 ns | 62.7 ns | +4.8 to +5.4 ns |\n\nBreak-even is at 2-3 fields. Either way, the difference is small next to the ~50 ns the log statement costs before the callback, and to what a consumer typically does with the entry (formatting, I/O).\n</details>\n\n",
